@@ -0,0 +1,74 @@
+# Carthage autoplay: worker, cargo and production coordination
+
+Reviewed against GitLab `5e6d03591731f64d589661f4f329aa28e8c71a7d`.
+These changes follow the separately documented source cleanup. Neither change
+alters the save layout or bundled DLL. Rebuild the DLL to use them in-game.
+
+## Corrected behavior
+
+* `AI_improveBonus`: permit replacing a non-connecting improvement on a revealed,
+  non-obsolete resource even with safe automation enabled. This matches the
+  resource exception already implemented in `AI_bestPlotBuild` and `groupBuild`.
+  Keep connecting improvements and acts-as-city improvements; keep actual build
+  legality and forest-preservation checks. The change does not allow arbitrary
+  rebuilding of non-resource plots. Autoplay retains the player's option flags.
+* The same resource selector now minimizes base build time directly. Previously
+  it minimized `10000 / (time + 1)`, favoring slower alternatives and losing
+  distinctions through integer rounding. Equal times retain XML iteration order.
+  This is not a full travel/progress/yield optimizer.
+* The two early settler-transport loading calls in `AI_cityDefenseMove` accept a
+  transport with any free capacity and no existing CITY_DEFENSE cargo. Previously
+  they required at most one free slot: RoM's four-slot galley containing just a
+  settler was rejected. Existing danger, best-garrison and subsequent minimum-
+  defense ordering remains intact. Other reinforcement paths remain unchanged.
+* Settler, explorer and naval production use aggregate supply APIs once. Those
+  APIs already include training units. A queued settler no longer consumes two
+  slots; a scout queued on a separate land area no longer suppresses local
+  demand. The generic explorer selector uses the common area-supply subtraction.
+  Redundant water-area queue additions are also removed; those counters are
+  often zero because ordinary ship production belongs to coastal land cities.
+* `AI_settleMove` returns after its danger branch issues MISSION_SKIP. Previously
+  it could continue to site selection, loading or founding in that invocation.
+
+## Evidence and limits
+
+`python3 Tools/test_ai_coordination.py` compiles actual methods/fragments as C++03
+with fatal UBSan, plus checks the relevant base XML. Eight tests cover:
+
+* 32 resource replacement combinations, including safe automation;
+* 2,401 build-time/legality combinations, plus forest and non-connecting controls;
+* 64 settler active/queued combinations;
+* 64 local/remote explorer combinations across both production paths;
+* 16 naval water/port queue combinations, including foreign-port ownership;
+* escort cargo gates at capacities 2 through 8 and the real departure predicate;
+* danger skip, retreat, safety and no-danger control paths;
+* four-slot galley, camp/mine Copper connection and civic XML facts.
+
+Seven tests reject the pre-fix GitLab source through native assertion failures;
+the XML control already passes. Two old lexical assertions demanded the extra
+queue queries that caused duplicate accounting. They now check aggregate wiring;
+the new numerical tests execute that accounting.
+
+These are source-contract tests with narrow adapters. They do not load a save,
+execute the Windows DLL, resolve real paths, or prove that an expedition succeeds.
+The cargo test covers an available CITY_DEFENSE escort and a settler aboard a
+SETTLER_SEA transport. It does not prove the reported escort-only cargo sequence
+has the same cause. The safe-automation flag in the supplied save is unverified.
+
+## Remaining gameplay questions
+
+The existing early workboat priority already follows one physical defender, but
+can be bypassed by danger, turtle strategy, an existing settler's escort demand,
+or earlier special branches. Ocean-wide worker counts also do not establish
+timely service to this city's resources. Compare build sequences and first
+usable yields, not just priority weights.
+
+Pastoral is not pasture-only: relative to Hunter-Gatherer it removes -25 worker
+speed and -25 military production modifiers and improves the trade-yield modifier
+from -50 to -30. It introduces low upkeep and may cost anarchy. Its timing needs
+an economic counterfactual, including integer rounding and the current package.
+
+Naval hull demand still uses army/coastal-city proxies rather than named voyages.
+Settlement scoring still separates site quality, affordability, production,
+escort assignment and route execution. Improving these requires coordinated
+operation records and engine traces, not more unrelated hard priority branches.
